<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Syunik</h1><p class="st-meta">987 CE – 1170 CE · capital Kapan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A south Armenian mountain kingdom that outlasted the fall of Ani, home to the great monastery of Tatev.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siunia-dynasty">Siunid</a> family had governed the mountainous province of Syunik since antiquity. Around 987 Smbat, a prince of its Baghk branch, took the title king with the backing of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bagratid-armenia">Bagratid Armenia</a>, forming a kingdom in the southern highlands between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-sevan">Lake Sevan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aras-river">Araxes</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Kings ruled from fortresses such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapan">Kapan</a> over a land of deep valleys, mines and pasture. The monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatev-monastery">Tatev</a>, seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of Syunik, held many villages and grew into a centre of learning. The kingdom acknowledged the kings of Ani and later paid tribute to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a> while defending its fortresses.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Seljuk conquests stripped away fortress after fortress in the 12th century, and in 1170 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/eldiguzids">Eldiguzids</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghaberd">Baghaberd</a>, ending the kingdom. Local princely families such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orbelian-dynasty">Orbelians</a> later restored Armenian rule in Syunik under Georgian and Mongol overlords.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-syunik.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Syunik" width="320" height="287"><figcaption>Image: www.Armenica.org · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Kingdom_of_Syounik-Baghk,_1020-1166.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>987 CE – 1170 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kapan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Smbat I, Grigor I, Senekerim</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Armenian Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Armenian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/armenia">Armenia</a>, <a href="/countries/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Baghk, Kingdom of Kapan, Syunik</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1078&amp;focus=kingdom-of-syunik" aria-label="Kingdom of Syunik on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5828 773 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Syunik lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6148 1029L6069 986M6148 1029L6481 1127M6148 1029L6124 1028"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6069 986h0M6481 1127h0M6124 1028h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6148 1029h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6148 1029h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kapan. <a href="/atlas?year=1078&amp;focus=kingdom-of-syunik">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
